    <article>
      <h1>Medical Disclaimer & Clinical Guidance</h1>
      <p>
        All calculations, biometric estimators, reference charts, and visual interpretations provided across <strong>Real BMI</strong> are developed strictly for educational screening, general wellness awareness, and personal reference. They do not constitute clinical diagnosis, professional medical advice, personalized physical therapy prescription, or individualized dietetic intervention.
      </p>

      <div class="notice-box">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent);">Critical Emergency Medical Notice:</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0; color: #cbd5e1; font-size: 0.95rem;">
          If you are experiencing acute chest pain, shortness of breath, sudden dizziness, severe weakness, or any life-threatening medical emergency, stop using browser calculation tools immediately and contact your local emergency medical services or proceed to the nearest hospital emergency department.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Scope and Limitations of Anthropometric Screening</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No Substitute for Clinical Examination:</strong> Formulas like Quetelet’s Body Mass Index (BMI = kg/m²), Boer Lean Body Mass, or Devine Ideal Weight are population-level statistical tools. They cannot measure visceral adipose tissue distribution, bone mineral density, or systemic cardiovascular health.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Athletes and Muscular Individuals:</strong> Highly trained resistance athletes frequently produce elevated BMI values categorizing them as overweight or obese, despite maintaining healthy low body fat levels.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pregnancy, Pediatrics & Clinical Conditions:</strong> Standard adult BMI and calorie deficit equations should never be applied to pregnant individuals, nursing mothers, growing children, elderly individuals with sarcopenia, or individuals in recovery from eating disorders.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Independent Medical Guidance:</strong> Always consult a licensed medical doctor, registered dietitian, or certified exercise physiologist before commencing any calorie deficit, rigorous strength program, or dramatic dietary change.
        </li>
      </ul>

